(d) No taxicab company permit shall be issued to an applicant unless the applicant has a valid and current occupation tax certificate to do business in the City if an occupation tax certificate is required by this Code.
(e) The taxicab company permit shall expire at the end of one (1) year from the date of issue and must be renewed prior to that date.
(f) If the applicant is not a sole proprietor or individual, then all partners, officers, shareholders, directors, members, managers holding ten (10) percent or more interest in the company or entity shall be required to comply with the provisions of this section.
(g) All applicants must:
(1) Be at least twenty-one (21) years of age;
(2) Be a citizen of the United States or an alien admitted for permanent residence or who has otherwise been granted employment authorization by the United States Department of Homeland Security (DHS);
(3) Have not been convicted, pled guilty or Nolo Contendere, been on probation, parole, or been imprisoned for a period of five (5) years previous to the date of application, for the violation of any of the following offenses of the State of Georgia, of any other state, or of the United States: any felony; driving under the influence of drugs or alcohol; criminal solicitation to commit any of the offenses listed in this subsection; attempt to commit any of the offenses listed in this subsection; any misdemeanor crime of violence or theft; any misdemeanor crime of possession, sale or distribution of illegal drugs; or any crime involving moral turpitude. Discharge without court adjudication of guilt pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 42-8-62 shall not disqualify an applicant from applying for a permit;
(4) Provide a verifiable dispatch location staffed by company agents or employees, have a published telephone number and have sufficient parking to accommodate its taxicabs when not in use;
(5) Provide proof that the applicant has a dedicated two-way communication system with a dispatcher that personally mans such communication system during the entire time that the applicant's business is open for service, or that any taxicab is available for transportation of fee-paying passengers. The dedicated two-way communication system must be a Federal Communications Commission licensed two-way radio network. Citizens Band radios, ham radios, cell phones and text paging devices shall not qualify as a dedicated two-way communication system;
(6) File applications for the initial permitting and inspection of the taxicabs, with a list of all drivers that will be scheduled for driver permitting, indicating whether drivers are company employees or contract drivers. A copy of each contract driver's insurance coverage must be included in the application;
(7) Provide a copy of the applicant's rate schedule and the daily hours of operation; and
(8) Provide the name, address and telephone number of a responsible individual residing in DeKalb County who will be the registered agent for the purpose of service of process or receipt of citations.
(9) Companies are responsible for reporting any change in qualifications or other licensing or permitting information previously supplied to the City within ten (10) days of the change.
(Ord. No. 04-22, § 1(2), 10-18-04; Ord. No. 07-09, § 1, 12-5-07; Ord. No. 2015-09, § 1, 7-6-15)

1663:
For the purposes of submissions under section 29-5 only:
Acceptable for public art shall include the following:
(1) All forms of limited edition or one-of-a-kind original creations of visual art created by an artist;
(2) Project features or enhancements which are unique and produced by a professional artist such as benches and fountains;
(3) Murals or mosaics covering walls;
(4) Natural art work;
(5) Professional artist sculptures which can be free-standing, wall-supported and suspended and made of durable materials suitable to the site and the climate; and
(6) Other suitable artworks as presented to, and approved by, the sole decision of the Art Committee.
Artist shall be defined as an individual, or a group of people, who meets one or more of the following criteria:
(1) Realizes income through the sale, performance, publication, or commission of original works of art, is licensed to teach art in the state;
(2) Has previously exhibited, presented, performed or published original works of art in museums, galleries or other recognized art venues and publications;
(3) Has formal training or education in a field of art; and
(4) Has received awards or other forms of recognition from arts juries, arts grant panels, and similar entities for his/her/their artistic abilities or accomplishments.
City-owned art shall be defined as a work of art commissioned or acquired by the City for the purposes of display on city property.
Not acceptable for public art shall include:
(1) Business logos or art that incorporates a logo for the primary purpose of advertising a business;
(2) Art that incorporates a distinguishable product for the primary purpose of advertising a business;
(3) Directional elements such as super-graphics, signage or color coding except where these elements are integral parts of the original works of art;
(4) Mass-produced art objects, such as fountains, statutory objects or playground equipment;
(5) Reproduction by mechanical or other means of original works of art;
(6) Decorative ornamental or functional elements created by the project architect instead of an artist commissioned for this purpose;
(7) Electrical, water or mechanical service for activation of the works of art;
(8) Installations which would encourage lack of maintenance or are representative of lack of maintenance or blight on any property;
(9) Art exhibitions, performances, and educational activities;
(10) Security and publicity concerning the works of art; and
(11) Art used as signage.
Public art shall be defined as a work created by a visual artist as defined herein, as approved by the Art Commission created herein, for display in public space or visible from a public space as demonstrated by a public art easement.
(Ord. No. 2021-06, § 1, 1-20-21)

1665:
The Art Commission shall have the following authority and responsibility:
(1) Develop policies, procedures and guidelines to implement a public implementation plan;
(2) Appoint task forces/selection committees for projects;
(3) If requested, review and approve "projects plans" for proposed artworks that are acquired or commissioned with City funds and/or proposed for City-owned property;
(4) If requested, review and approve the selection of artists and artist concepts for artworks commissioned with City funds and/or proposed for City-owned property;
(5) Prepare an annual report to Council on public art activities;
(6) Review and recommend public art proposals consistent with definitions and criteria for public art and themes adopted by Mayor and City Council;
(7) If requested, recommend the acceptance of donations of public art for public property; and
(8) Review and recommend public art recommendations in the comprehensive plans, area plans, overlay districts and development conditions.
(9) In conjunction with the staff and City Attorney, to:
a. Approve to form, substance, and requirements of all applications and/or documents required for submission of proposed projects pursuant to section 29-5 of this chapter, and
b. Determine the date to begin with the acceptance of proposals pursuant to section 29-5.
(Ord. No. 2021-06, § 1, 1-20-21)

1666:
The role of Mayor and City Council shall be as follows:
(1) Review and approve a public art master plan;
(2) Confirm appointments to the Art Commission;
(3) Review and approve public art projects after approval by Art Commission and determine whether the City will financially co-sponsor any such projects (only if on right of way or established city-owned property);
(4) Review and approve acquisitions, loans, donations and de-accessions upon referral by the Art Commission; and
(5) Develop approved themes, requirements, and locations for public art project submissions.
(Ord. No. 2021-06, § 1, 1-20-21)

1667:
(a) Any artist, developer or Doraville property owner may submit an application for a public art installation in the City to the Economic Development Department. The application, which shall be on a form created by the Economic Development Department, shall provide the following information:
(1) Name and resume of the proposed artist, including photographic or video representations of artist's existing work, preferably works of public art;
(2) Artist rendering of the proposed public art installation, including specific design elements, medium, materials, and description of any work that will require local, state or federal permits.
(3) The suggested location of installation and description of why said location is appropriate for this public art installation, as well as affidavit from the property owner with consent for said installation.
(4) The desired length of time said public art installation will remain on the property (private property only);
(5) Demonstration of property rights to install proposed public art, the proposed cost of installing same, and demonstration of the existence of the funds for said installation;
(6) Proposed agreement between property owner and the City requiring property owner to erect and maintain the public art installation at the property owner's expense for the life of the installation;
(7) How the public art installation meets the theme(s) and other criteria approved by the Mayor and City Council; and
(8) A proposed and executed public art easement which shall grant the City an easement in the location of the public art and grant the City all authority to, in the City's discretion, maintain said installation and claim ownership in said installation (private property only);
(9) Agreement by the artist that the City shall have the unfettered right, in its sole discretion, without notice to, approval of, or consultation with artist, to (i) relocate the artwork to another site or location, (ii) discontinue any required maintenance, if any, of the artwork, (iii) disassemble and remove the artwork in its entirety, or (iv) alter the artwork in connection with emergency and/or public safety considerations. Artist shall acknowledge and agree: (i) that City must retain the freedoms set forth in this section with respect to the artwork for public policy, municipal planning, public safety, environmental, budgetary and related reasons, (ii) that City's retention of such freedoms is reasonable under the circumstances.
(10) Agreement by the artist that the City may make any material alteration or change to the artwork in a manner that would affect the artwork's intended character or appearance so long as the artist has been provided notice of such proposed alteration and, if requested by artist following such notice, the opportunity to provide reasonable input in an effort to accommodate any aesthetic or other concerns of artist with respect to any such proposed alteration.
(11) Subject to the foregoing limitations of paragraphs (9) and (10), agreement that the artist waives any rights he or she may have regarding the Visual Artists Rights Act of 1990, 17 USC Section 106A.
(12) Any other information as determined by the Economic Development Department and the Art Commission necessary for determination to accept or reject the installation.
(b) Upon submitting the application, the Economic Development Department shall forward the application to the Art Commission which shall consider same in an upcoming regular or special meeting taking place at least thirty (30) days after the date of submission of the application. The Economic Development Department shall place a sign next to the proposed location with the date and time of the Art Commission meeting and a picture of the proposed installation.
(c) The Art Commission shall consider the proposed installation on the basis of whether:
(1) The application is complete and proper in all respects;
(2) The proposal meets the theme(s) and requirements approved by the Mayor and City Council;
(3) The suggested location is appropriate for the proposed, or any, public art installation;
(4) The medium and materials utilized for the installations are appropriate and desirable;
(5) The proposal meets all of the criteria and requirements as set forth in this chapter, and
(6) And any other considerations the Committee finds relevant to its determination on the application.
The Art Commission shall approve or deny the public art installation proposal pursuant to the above criteria in its sole discretion. The decision of the Art Commission shall be final and not appealable.
(d) If the Art Commission approves the proposed public art installation, they shall forward the application to the Mayor and City Council, which shall review the proposed maintenance agreement and public art easement and, upon recommendation of the City Attorney, approve or disapprove the maintenance agreement and public art easement on the basis of whether they serve the purpose for maintenance and existence of the installation as approved public art.
(Ord. No. 2021-06, § 1, 1-20-21)
